WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
- Thanks to Ting Mobile for
keeping legal legal in the air.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.750
In the waning days of
the Trump presidency,

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.150
people are wondering if president Trump

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:08.900
is going to exercise the pardon power

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.420
to pardon himself and his family.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.000
Well, yeah, probably.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.053
Good night everyone.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.140
Oh, you want to know
if there are any limits

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.630
on the presidential pardon power,

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:20.910
or if there's anything that can be done

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.180
to stop the president using
the pardon power for himself

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.610
and potentially co-conspirators.

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:28.440
Well, the US Constitution
gives the president the power

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.030
to grant reprieves and pardons

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.370
for offenses against the United States,

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:35.280
except in cases of impeachment,
even pardon the Turkey.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.890
- Drumstick.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.180
You are here by pardoned.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.400
- But could President
Trump pardon this Turkey?

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.360
Could he pardon his family
in potential co-conspirators?

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.910
Could he step down and
have President Mike Pence

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:49.874
pardon him before he leaves office?

00:00:49.874 --> 00:00:52.957
(introductory music)

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.450
Hey LegalEagle, It's time
to think like a lawyer

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:57.710
for the president.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.810
The pardon power is kind
of like a magic eraser

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.310
that sets aside punishment
for federal crimes.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.620
But that doesn't mean that
there are no limits whatsoever

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.330
to the power to pardon.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:07.300
The text of Article II, Section 2,

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.170
establishes a few
limitations to be broad grant

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.450
of pardon power.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:13.490
For example, the pardon
power does not apply

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:14.323
to state crimes.

00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:16.610
Why? Because the
constitution explicitly says

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:18.720
that, "the president may only give pardons

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.570
to offenses against the United States."

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.840
And that means federal crimes.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.220
People often mistakenly
believe a president can pardon

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.310
or commute the sentences

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.510
of people convicted of state
crimes, but that can't be done.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:31.410
People with state convictions have to look

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.420
to their governors for reprieve.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.080
The pardon power also cannot be used

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.840
to set aside impeachment.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.320
There is some disagreement
about what this really means,

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.830
but at a minimum, the president
cannot pardon an executive

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:45.040
or judicial officer from
an impeachment conviction.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.490
In all likelihood, if President
Trump had been convicted

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.254
in the Senate, he could
not have pardoned himself

00:01:49.254 --> 00:01:51.340
in order to remain in office.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.940
And depending on how the
conviction was stylized

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.270
by the Senate and the particular
punishment that was given,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.110
the conviction would
have immediately removed

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:00.560
the presidential power anyway.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.220
But there's still some
ambiguity about the phrase

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:04.590
in cases of impeachment as a limit

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.620
on a presidential pardon power.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.120
Especially for president pardons,

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:11.530
people who helped him
commit impeachable offenses.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.370
Some people believe that
the constitution imposes

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:14.979
other limits on the pardon power.

00:02:14.979 --> 00:02:16.930
They generally pointed
the president's Article II

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.029
duties to faithfully execute the laws.

00:02:19.029 --> 00:02:20.970
When a president takes the oath of office,

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:23.850
he or she must affirm their
commitment to the rule of law.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.960
So we could argue that a
president who pardons himself

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.680
has co-conspirators or
accepts favors in exchange

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.210
for pardons is not
faithfully executing the laws

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.430
or the duties of the office as a whole.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.080
These arguments certainly make sense,

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.600
but they're completely untested

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:39.470
and could be completely unconstitutional.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:41.190
The most common response would probably be

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.040
that if a president is acting
as if they're above the law,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.870
then the remedy is impeachment

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.140
and the founders wrote
at least one limitation

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.040
into the pardon power.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.040
If they wanted to write more,

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.210
they could have put in
those limitations directly

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:54.220
into the document.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.199
But let's take a step back
and talk about the origins

00:02:56.199 --> 00:02:57.293
of pardon power.

00:02:57.293 --> 00:03:00.170
And the pardon power was adopted
from an English tradition

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.860
known as the Royal
Prerogative of English Kings,

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.740
which gave the King the ability
to overturn any sentence.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:06.760
It might seem counterintuitive

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.780
that the American founders
adopted the pardon

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.110
since they had just revolted
against King George III,

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.880
because they thought
he had too much power.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.227
And James Madison wrote that,

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:16.180
"giving one person legislative, executive

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:18.950
and judicial powers was the
very definition of tyranny."

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.280
So why does the constitution
give the president the ability

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.240
to quickly reverse a court's judgment?

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:25.770
Well, ask Alexander Hamilton,

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:27.100
who argued that the pardon power

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.440
was a tool a president could use

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.060
to swiftly diffuse tensions
during times of national crisis.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:34.200
George Washington immediately
use the pardon power

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.440
to end the Whiskey Rebellion.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.290
And giving the tax
protesting farmers a pardon

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:39.950
rather than sentencing them to death

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.670
actually worked out pretty well.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.370
But Hamilton trusted the pardon

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.250
because he thought it would be used only

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:47.130
by the most virtuous of men.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.620
But we are a long, long way
from George Washington now.

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:52.870
But one of the issues
that perennially comes up,

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:56.290
especially in the context
of a potential Trump pardon

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.220
is whether a pardon is
an admission of guilt.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:01.150
If someone accepts a
pardon, does he or she admit

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:03.679
that they were guilty
of the underlying crime?

00:04:03.679 --> 00:04:05.120
Whether accepting a pardon

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.600
is an admission of guilt under the law

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.030
is actually an open question.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:11.500
In the 1800s after the Civil
War in Ex parte Garland,

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:12.667
the Supreme Court said that,

00:04:12.667 --> 00:04:15.030
"a pardon blots out
the existence of guilt,

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.900
so that in the eyes of
the law the offender

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:19.570
is as innocent as if he'd
never committed the offense."

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.690
This sentence suggests
that a pardon wipes away

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.050
any person's guilt,

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.260
yet the court seemed to back
off of this interpretation

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.490
in the 1915 case, Burdick
versus the United States.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.830
In Burdick, the court
observed that a confession

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:34.280
of guilt may be implied in
the acceptance of a pardon.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:35.850
But Burdick was about whether a recipient

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.330
could turn down a pardon.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.200
As some have argued, the court meant

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.380
that as a practical matter,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.650
because pardons make people look guilty.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.870
A recipient might not want to accept one,

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.520
but arguably pardons have
no formal legal effect

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.770
of declaring guilt.

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.210
In fact, many people have been
granted pardon specifically,

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.540
because they're innocent.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.700
There isn't much case
law directly on point,

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.890
but recent lower court cases seem to align

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:59.180
with Burdick statement
that a person admits guilt

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:00.920
when they accept a pardon.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.740
President George H.W Bush
pardoned Elliott Abrams

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:06.180
for his role in the Iran-Contra scandal.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.640
But the DC bar initiated
disciplinary proceedings

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.420
against Abrams for lying to Congress

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.880
during its investigation of that scandal.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.770
Abrams challenged the
proceedings on the grounds

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:17.502
that the pardon not only erased
his criminal convictions,

00:05:17.502 --> 00:05:20.010
but it meant he couldn't
be sanctioned by the bar

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:20.930
for lying under oath.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.457
The DC circuit disagreed holding,

00:05:23.457 --> 00:05:25.720
"because a pardon does not blot out guilt,

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.480
one can conclude that a pardon

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.020
does not blot out probable cause

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.990
or guilt, or expungement indictment."

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:32.810
So the pardon didn't force the DC bar

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:34.490
to treat Abrams as lying under oath

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:36.020
is something that never happened.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.090
But could the president issue a pardon

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:39.880
that is so open-ended and broad

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.710
that it doesn't actually
specify who the recipient is?

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.680
Could a president issue a mass pardon

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.170
to all people who meet certain criteria?

00:05:47.170 --> 00:05:49.670
The answer to that is probably yes,

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.980
there's no limitation on broad pardons.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:53.710
For example, Andrew Johnson pardoned

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.870
all ex Confederate soldiers in 1868.

00:05:56.870 --> 00:05:59.500
Benjamin Harrison, pardoned all
members of the Mormon church

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:00.810
who had committed polygamy.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.500
And Jimmy Carter, pardoned
all who had evaded

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.640
the Vietnam War in 1977.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.900
So if historical precedent
is any indication,

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.550
if President Trump wanted to pardon,

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.920
let's say every person who
had ever been convicted

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.690
of a federal drug crime,
he could probably do it.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:18.110
He does not have to specify
each individual person by name.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.890
But by the same token,

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:22.500
he could probably specify
that anyone involved

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:25.361
in any crime related to the "Russia Hoax"

00:06:25.361 --> 00:06:27.030
could also receive a pardon

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:29.670
and effectively end
investigations of that.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:30.990
Which brings us to another one

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.160
of the perennial questions about pardons,

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.550
which is can a president
grant a perspective pardon

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.010
where there's been no conviction

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.070
or even indictment for a crime?

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:41.430
We expect President Trump
to issue many pardons

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.890
on his way out of office.

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.240
But can he pardon people
who have yet to be indicted

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.200
or even investigated for a federal crime?

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.110
And the answer to that is a
clear, yes, he can do that.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:51.983
It appears that the
president can issue a pardon

00:06:51.983 --> 00:06:53.600
before any criminal proceeding

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.760
against that person has been initiated.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.620
This is a prospective pardon.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:00.230
And in the 1866 case, Ex parte Garland

00:07:00.230 --> 00:07:01.380
that we just talked about,

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.437
the Supreme Court announced
that the pardon power,

00:07:03.437 --> 00:07:05.940
"extends to every offense known to the law

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.260
and may be exercised at the
time after its commission,

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.440
either before legal proceedings are taken,

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:15.070
or during the pendency or
after conviction and judgment."

00:07:15.070 --> 00:07:17.440
This basically means that
the president only needs

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.340
to know a crime has been committed.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.360
He doesn't have to wait until
after the authorities indict

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:24.940
or arrest a person in order
to grant them a pardon.

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:26.690
The most famous perspective pardon

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:29.400
is when President Gerald
Ford pardoned Richard Nixon

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.170
for, "all offenses
against the United States,

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:34.610
which he has committed
or may have committed

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.590
or taken part in during the period

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:41.510
from January 20th, 1969
through August 9th, 1974."

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:43.780
Nixon had not been indicted for any crimes

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.260
and resigned before he
could even be impeached.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.050
The pardon shielded him
from any future prosecution

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:51.330
based on any criminal
acts he might've committed

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:53.837
during his time specified in the pardon.

00:07:53.837 --> 00:07:57.760
But that takes us to a more
insidious potentials situation,

00:07:57.760 --> 00:07:59.620
a quid pro quo pardon.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:02.350
The phrase quid pro quo,
as you probably now know

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:06.080
is something for something
or give and take.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:08.030
So can a president dole out pardons

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:10.040
to people who offer him gifts, money,

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:11.630
or other things of value?

00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:13.780
Well, the law at 18 U.S.C 201

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:16.320
makes it illegal to
bribe a public official.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:17.500
Now it's an open question,

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:19.010
whether it applies to the president.

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:20.880
But putting that aside for the moment,

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.580
the bribery statute doesn't necessarily

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:24.950
make a quid pro quo pardon illegal.

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:27.480
In 2020, the House Democrats
introduced legislation

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:29.180
that would make it illegal for a president

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:32.030
to give a pardon in exchange
for something of value.

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:34.500
The same proposed legislation
also made it illegal

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.430
for a president to pardon himself.

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:38.350
Although it's sort of an open question,

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:41.780
whether this law itself
would be constitutional.

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:45.050
The Congress can't pass
a law that conflicts

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:46.930
with the constitution itself.

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.790
Interestingly, Bill Barr,
yes, that Bill Barr,

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:53.290
is on record as saying that a
president who sells a pardon

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:56.630
is guilty of a crime, quid pro quo crime.

00:08:56.630 --> 00:09:01.630
- Do you believe a president
could lawfully issue a pardon

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:04.070
in exchange for the recipient's promise

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:05.503
to not incriminate him?

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:08.270
- No, that would be a crime.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.473
- And he went on to
say that if a president

00:09:10.473 --> 00:09:13.650
gave away a pardon in
exchange for someone's silence

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:15.420
that could be obstruction of justice.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.470
- Oh, a quid pro quo to change testimony

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:18.970
could potentially be obstruction.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:20.790
- And that makes sense because
there's plenty of things

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:23.030
that someone can do as
a governmental employee

00:09:23.030 --> 00:09:24.500
that you're authorized to do,

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.540
but under certain
circumstances can be illegal.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:29.780
For example, if your job is
to sell military equipment

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:32.480
if you choose to sell military
equipment to North Korea.

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.320
Well you're authorized to do
it but it can still be a crime.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:37.570
And selling a pardon as president

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:39.742
is one of those classic
examples that you use to say

00:09:39.742 --> 00:09:41.880
that yes, the president has that authority

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:43.790
but there are circumstances
that can give rise

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:45.220
to illegality.

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:48.300
Now query what would happen
under those circumstances

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:49.530
in terms of whether the pardon

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:51.358
would still be in effect or not.

00:09:51.358 --> 00:09:53.640
These are things that
would need to be litigated

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:55.807
and hopefully they'll
never need to be litigated.

00:09:55.807 --> 00:09:58.530
But the bottom line is
that the most likely remedy

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:01.350
for a quid pro quo pardon
is probably impeachment,

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.090
not criminal prosecution.

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:05.530
And speaking of quid pro quo.(laughs)

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:06.840
I was checking my YouTube stats

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:09.810
and only 30% of this audience
is subscribed to this channel.

00:10:09.810 --> 00:10:12.620
So if you're getting value,
the quid from this channel,

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.680
please subscribe and
hit the bell, the quo.

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:15.899
And as the president says,

00:10:15.899 --> 00:10:18.400
but first I'd like you
to do me this favor.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.670
Which takes us to the question

00:10:19.670 --> 00:10:22.350
of whether the president
can pardon his own family.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.570
And the answer there is probably yes.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:25.790
There's no law or rule

00:10:25.790 --> 00:10:28.410
that says President Trump can't
pardon his family members.

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:30.470
In 2001, former President Bill Clinton

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:32.840
pardoned his brother,
Roger, who was convicted

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:35.040
for cocaine possession in Arkansas.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:37.530
But there's nothing in the
text of the constitution

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.560
or the case law surrounding
the pardon power

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:40.840
that would seem like it would limit

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:43.860
President Trump's ability to
pardon members of his family,

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.380
whether it appears proper or not.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:48.520
But if you're talking about
President Trump's family

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.290
that's a natural segue way to the question

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:53.320
of whether the president can
pardon his co-conspirators.

00:10:53.320 --> 00:10:55.950
President Trump has been
implicated in many federal crimes

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:58.440
that took place before
and after he took office.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:01.830
We've done several videos
about that in the past.

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:03.800
And in one particular instance,

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:06.070
President Trump's former
lawyer and fixer, Michael Cohen

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:07.590
was sentenced to three years in prison

00:11:07.590 --> 00:11:10.380
for several crimes, including
using campaign funds

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.110
to pay off women who had
affairs with the president.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.720
Federal Judge, William H
Pauley III said in open court

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:19.330
that Trump had directed Cohen
to commit a federal felony.

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:21.310
Trump was referred to in this proceedings

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:24.220
as individual number one and
unindicted co-conspirator.

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:25.790
And that's just one particular instance.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:27.570
There are lots of the
president's associates

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.110
who have been implicated in crimes

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.640
that he was either
involved in or directed.

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:34.060
Can the president pardon these people?

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:36.050
Well, it's certainly
possible that President Trump

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:39.060
will pardon people who are
considered his co-conspirators.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:42.110
And I hate to tell you, but
it's actually happened before.

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:45.130
In 1987, an independent counsel
was investigating crimes

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:46.900
committed by members of
the Reagan Administration

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:49.980
for violating federal law
by selling weapons to Iran.

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:51.480
And then illegally funneling the money

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.930
to Contras in Nicaragua.

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:54.170
- For 18 months now,

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:57.530
we have had underway a secret
diplomatic initiative to Iran.

00:11:57.530 --> 00:11:58.790
- For all you kids out there,

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:01.470
this was the Iran-Contra scandal.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:03.180
Attorney General William Barr,

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:06.652
yes, that Bill BARR helped
President George H.W Bush

00:12:06.652 --> 00:12:09.470
stop this investigation
by pardoning six people,

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:12.360
including former Defense
Secretary, Caspar Weinberger,

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:13.830
who was about to go on trial

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.750
to face charges about lying to Congress.

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.280
So you can chalk this one
up to the pardon power

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:20.530
being very, very broad.

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:23.070
There's probably nothing
that would stop the president

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.198
from pardoning people
who have committed crimes

00:12:25.198 --> 00:12:27.630
at the president's own behest.

00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:30.770
And of course the first but
not the last lame duck pardon

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:33.470
has been given to former
general Michael Flynn

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:35.480
in an insanely broad pardon.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.230
I've covered the Michael Flynn situation

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:39.510
in the past in a different
video, you can check that out.

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:41.507
But many people are
outraged by the Flynn pardon

00:12:41.507 --> 00:12:43.650
and what arguably makes this different

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:45.440
from some of President
Trump's other pardons

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:47.921
is that the criminal
conduct lying to the FBI

00:12:47.921 --> 00:12:50.940
was arguably designed to
protect President Trump himself

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:52.910
from prosecution or impeachment.

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:55.360
So here we have Trump pardoning a person

00:12:55.360 --> 00:12:59.130
who may have helped him
hide his criminal conduct.

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:00.730
Does this actually matter though,

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:03.900
when it comes to assessing the
legality of the Flynn burden?

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:05.120
Probably not.

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:07.270
Although law professors
like Mark Ostler have said

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:10.440
the Flynn pardon is a despicable
use of an awesome power.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:13.220
Even he agrees that this is
part of the broad expression

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:15.490
of the pardon power presidents enjoy.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:18.090
That to be clear, this
is bad news for democracy

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:20.740
if a president can use
pardons the way a mafia don

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:23.290
would wield them as a
carrot to ensure loyalty

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:24.950
from the don soldiers.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:28.007
But if you have a problem with
it, amend the constitution.

00:13:28.007 --> 00:13:31.180
No, seriously, amend the constitution.

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:33.580
President Trump could
pardon Jared Kushner's dad,

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:36.470
Steve Bannon, Paul Manafort,
and even Rudy Giuliani,

00:13:36.470 --> 00:13:37.880
who's allegedly being investigated

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.460
for breaking lobbying laws.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:42.450
But does Congress just have
to sit back and take it?

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:45.200
Can Congress investigate
the use of a pardon?

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.920
Although Congress probably lacks

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:48.120
direct legislative authority

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:50.840
to limit the pardon power
via federal statute,

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:53.083
it does have other constitutional
tools for regulating

00:13:53.083 --> 00:13:55.680
and overseeing the
president's pardon authority.

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:57.780
Congress has the right to
use its oversight powers

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:58.613
to investigate.

00:13:58.613 --> 00:14:00.700
They can issue subpoenas and hold hearings

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.720
on the matters of presidential pardons.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:05.630
Admittedly, it's sort of an
open question in this era

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:07.990
just how powerful
congressional subpoenas are,

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:09.460
and whether Congress has the ability

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:11.810
to enforce those subpoenas
against the executive.

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:15.170
But I suspect that out of the wash

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:17.390
the courts will affirm Congress's ability

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:20.080
to enforce subpoenas
into matters like these.

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:23.130
And when President Bill Clinton
pardoned about 450 people

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:24.690
at the end of his second term,

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:26.500
Republicans and Congress were outraged.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:28.180
And many of these pardons
were controversial,

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:31.450
such as his pardon of Democratic
party donor, Marc Rich,

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:34.360
who had fled the country
because of tax evasion charges.

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:36.500
But congressional committees
held oversight hearings

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.310
to assess whether these pardons were

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.410
for lack of a better word, bad.

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:43.260
But the bottom line was that
even if the pardons looked bad

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:46.520
or rewarded people who had
connections to President Clinton,

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:47.920
Congress lacked the authority

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:50.500
to do anything about
whatever it uncovered.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:52.410
Although presidents have
complied with subpoenas

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:54.230
and witnesses have testified.

00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:55.570
The Department of Justice has said

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:57.210
that such compliance was voluntary

00:14:57.210 --> 00:14:59.650
and could not necessarily
be compelled by a court.

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:01.720
It would certainly be
an interesting test case

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:02.570
for the Supreme Court,

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:04.750
which has expressed some
skepticism about the scope

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:07.210
of how subpoenas into President
Trump's financial records.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:10.630
But it seems like it has to
be the case that if the remedy

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:14.110
for improper use of a
pardon is impeachment,

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:16.200
then Congress has to have the ability

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:19.980
to investigate the use of those pardons.

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:22.570
But that takes us to the penultimate issue

00:15:22.570 --> 00:15:23.840
with respect to pardons.

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:28.020
Which is, what would happen if
President Trump stepped down

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.270
and allowed Mike Pence to pardon him?

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:33.900
Many people wonder if Donald
Trump could resign at 11:59 AM

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:37.140
on January the 20th, making
Mike Pence the president

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:38.770
for just a few minutes,

00:15:38.770 --> 00:15:41.820
just long enough to
pardon President Trump.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:43.900
Yes, this is the scenario that led

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.240
to Gerald Ford's pardon of Richard Nixon.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:48.641
For years, people have claimed that Nixon

00:15:48.641 --> 00:15:51.170
and Ford made a deal
that Nixon would resign

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:53.400
if Ford agreed to pardon him.

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:55.220
This sounds like a sleazy deal,

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:57.330
but there doesn't seem to be
any evidence of wrongdoing

00:15:57.330 --> 00:15:59.230
or shady dealing by Ford.

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:00.630
But at the end of the day,

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:02.400
Trump could effectively
do the same thing instead

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:04.770
of leaving office two
years before his term

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.690
is up as Richard Nixon did.

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:08.840
Trump could effectively step down

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:10.740
with just a few minutes
left in his presidency,

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:11.783
turn it over to Mike Pence

00:16:11.783 --> 00:16:16.400
and ask for a pardon from
President Mike Pence.

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:18.180
Of course, President
Trump has already stated

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:19.330
that he could pardon himself.

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:21.427
So he doesn't actually need to do that.

00:16:21.427 --> 00:16:22.720
But the question is,

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:25.840
can the president actually pardon himself?

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:27.900
President Trump certainly
thinks he has that power.

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:29.027
He said on more than one occasion,

00:16:29.027 --> 00:16:31.540
"I have the absolute
right to pardon myself."

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:32.760
But it's an open question,

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:35.220
because obviously no one
has ever done this before.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:36.780
So it's uncharted territory.

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:39.180
And as with most things
with constitutional law.

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:41.580
First, we look to the
text of the constitution.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:42.445
And as we've discussed,

00:16:42.445 --> 00:16:45.130
the constitution gives
the president the power

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:46.300
to grant reprieves and pardon

00:16:46.300 --> 00:16:48.000
for offenses against the United States,

00:16:48.000 --> 00:16:50.800
except in cases of impeachment.

00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:53.150
So at first blush, it
doesn't contain a limitation

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:55.350
that says, unless you are the President

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:57.150
of the United States.

00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:59.450
But that's not necessarily
the end of the question.

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:01.950
In particular, because
I think a lot of people

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:03.850
consider the idea of a
president pardoning him

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:05.450
or herself to be so odious.

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:06.630
Another prudential argument

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:08.680
is that if the president
could pardon himself,

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:10.970
he would forever be above the law.

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:13.513
Many believe that a sitting
president cannot be indicted.

00:17:13.513 --> 00:17:15.110
If the president could pardon himself

00:17:15.110 --> 00:17:16.090
before he leaves office,

00:17:16.090 --> 00:17:18.680
he'd be immune from all
federal crimes while in office

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:20.430
and then forever when he leaves.

00:17:20.430 --> 00:17:22.490
And in at least one part
of the constitution,

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:24.371
the framers clearly
intended criminal penalties

00:17:24.371 --> 00:17:25.700
after office.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:27.337
Article I, Section 3 declares,

00:17:27.337 --> 00:17:28.790
"judgment in cases of impeachment

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:31.290
shall not extend further
than to removal from office

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:33.440
and disqualification to
hold and enjoy any office

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:35.820
of honor, trust or profit
on the United States.

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:38.710
But the party convicted
shall nevertheless be liable

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:41.760
and subject to indictment,
trial, judgment and punishment,

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.540
according to law."

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:45.680
Many argue that the constitution says that

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:47.630
the Congress may not
impose criminal penalties

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:49.240
on a president, but the president

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:52.311
is still nonetheless subject
to federal criminal prosecution

00:17:52.311 --> 00:17:54.050
through ordinary courts

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:56.750
and allowing a self
pardon would eviscerate

00:17:56.750 --> 00:17:58.330
that clause of the constitution.

00:17:58.330 --> 00:18:00.010
And one of the canons of construction,

00:18:00.010 --> 00:18:03.400
the rule against surplusage
would lend credence to the idea

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:05.550
that a self-dealing pardon is void.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:07.530
Other strict textualist
argue that the plain meaning

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:09.010
of the pardon clause
doesn't actually allow

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:10.110
for a self-dealing pardon,

00:18:10.110 --> 00:18:11.930
specifically because of the word, grant,

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:13.350
as it's used in that clause.

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:14.183
The constitution says,

00:18:14.183 --> 00:18:16.590
"the president shall have
power to grant reprieves

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:19.240
and pardons for offenses
against the United States."

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:21.960
The grant of a pardon
implies the transferring

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:24.000
of one thing to another.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.510
The constitution uses the
word, grant five other times,

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:28.150
always in the context of one person

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:31.640
or body granting something
to another person or body.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:32.967
For example, Article I says,

00:18:32.967 --> 00:18:36.340
"the legislative power herein
granted shall be vested

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:38.520
in the Congress of the United States."

00:18:38.520 --> 00:18:40.130
Here the people of the United States

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:42.770
are granting Congress
the right to make laws.

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:44.920
Also, Article I says, "Congress may grant

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:46.600
letters of mark and reprisal."

00:18:46.600 --> 00:18:48.420
This man Congress can give private ships

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:51.040
the right to start wars
against ships of other nations.

00:18:51.040 --> 00:18:53.170
This was an important grant
of power back in the day

00:18:53.170 --> 00:18:54.630
since it protected private vessels

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.700
against accusations of piracy.

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:58.950
Notice again, that Congress
is transferring a thing,

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.940
letters of mark and
reprisal to another entity.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:05.300
It seems to imply to people
or at least a separate grantor

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:07.950
and the grantee, kind
of like a kidney donor.

00:19:07.950 --> 00:19:09.510
You can have the power to give a kidney

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:11.312
but you can't give it to yourself.

00:19:11.312 --> 00:19:14.150
LegalEageley speaking,
that's not a good idea,

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:16.020
but I'm skeptical of this argument.

00:19:16.020 --> 00:19:18.314
For one, regular people and lawyers

00:19:18.314 --> 00:19:21.250
do use grants in a reflexive way.

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:22.740
For example in property law,

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.270
a person might grant him
or herself an easement

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:26.470
or a life estate.

00:19:26.470 --> 00:19:29.480
A life estate is a property
that an individual owns

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.860
only through the duration
of their lifetime.

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:33.650
You don't see life estates all that often,

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:35.270
but I assure you they do exist.

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:36.360
And law students hate them

00:19:36.360 --> 00:19:38.663
because they make property
law really complicated

00:19:38.663 --> 00:19:39.940
and annoying.

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:42.540
But the point is you can
grant yourself a life estate.

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:45.430
So it's not clear which
way this argument would go.

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:46.596
Which is one of the reasons why

00:19:46.596 --> 00:19:49.537
the Office of Legal
Counsel of the White House

00:19:49.537 --> 00:19:53.130
has opined on this particular
issue several times.

00:19:53.130 --> 00:19:54.880
And there's a general principle in U.S law

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:58.014
that a person cannot be the
judge in their own case.

00:19:58.014 --> 00:20:01.660
Which would argue against
the president being able

00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:03.080
to pardon him or herself.

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:04.982
And in 1974, when President Nixon

00:20:04.982 --> 00:20:07.890
asked the Office of Legal Counsel, the OLC

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:10.530
to determine if he could pardon himself,

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:12.180
the OLC relied on that principle

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:14.990
in saying that President Nixon
could not pardon himself.

00:20:14.990 --> 00:20:18.860
And in the famous August
5th, 1974 memo of the OLC,

00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:22.247
Mary Lawton, the acting
assistant attorney general wrote,

00:20:22.247 --> 00:20:25.990
"under the fundamental rule
that no one may be the judge

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:28.620
in their own case, the president
cannot pardon himself."

00:20:28.620 --> 00:20:31.437
And four days later,
Nixon resigned in disgrace

00:20:31.437 --> 00:20:33.730
possibly because he didn't think

00:20:33.730 --> 00:20:36.380
that he was legally
allowed to pardon himself.

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:38.920
So given the President Nixon staff opined

00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:40.560
that he could not pardon himself,

00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:42.820
and often people complain that the OLC

00:20:42.820 --> 00:20:45.430
is really beholden to the presidency

00:20:45.430 --> 00:20:48.450
and aren't really giving
objective opinions.

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:50.920
And even Nixon himself would not go so far

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:52.790
to issue a self pardon.

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:53.893
That's potentially persuasive authority

00:20:53.893 --> 00:20:56.920
that the president doesn't
have the power to self pardon.

00:20:56.920 --> 00:20:59.350
And there is some authority
from the Supreme Court

00:20:59.350 --> 00:21:02.170
that's persuasive because the
Supreme Court hasn't ruled

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:05.038
on this precise issue of the
president pardoning himself.

00:21:05.038 --> 00:21:08.940
So it's not dispositive, but
it might help in this analysis.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:11.230
There's really only one Supreme
Court case that's on point,

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:14.070
and that is Ex parte
Garland case from 1866,

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:17.040
which we've talked about now
twice in this same video,

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:18.717
because there aren't that many cases.

00:21:18.717 --> 00:21:21.600
And the Ex parte Garland case
concerned Congress's ability

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:23.400
to exclude members of the Confederacy

00:21:23.400 --> 00:21:26.830
from practicing federal
law after the Civil War

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:28.940
and whether the president could pardon

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:30.510
a Confederate soldier.

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:32.000
Again, going back to the idea

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:34.760
of whether the pardon expunges everything,

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:37.060
or whether there's some remaining residue

00:21:37.060 --> 00:21:39.660
from the prior crimes
that have been committed.

00:21:39.660 --> 00:21:41.530
And the Supreme Court held five to four

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:44.140
that the president could
pardon the Confederate Senator.

00:21:44.140 --> 00:21:46.550
But more importantly, the
court held unanimously

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:47.920
that the Supreme Court has the authority

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:50.600
to interpret the scope
of the pardon power.

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:52.470
And like the bedrock Supreme Court case

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:54.120
of Marbury versus Madison,

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:56.390
Ex parte Garland is
probably less important

00:21:56.390 --> 00:21:58.310
for the actual outcome of the case.

00:21:58.310 --> 00:21:59.810
Then for the principle
that the Supreme Court

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:02.480
has the overview authority
to interpret the constitution

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:05.060
in this case and make a
decision one way or the other

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:07.320
about the president's pardon power.

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.230
So this probably means
that if President Trump

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:11.420
tried to pardon himself,

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:13.920
he could do it in the first instance.

00:22:13.920 --> 00:22:16.050
He might be able to file
the paperwork for himself,

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:17.946
but under a Supreme Court case law,

00:22:17.946 --> 00:22:21.110
the Supreme Court has the
power to review that pardon.

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:23.210
And it's possible that the Supreme Court

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:26.320
could review the case and actually nullify

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:27.300
the president's self pardon

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:29.730
on the grounds that the
president cannot be the judge

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:32.890
in his own case or that the
impeachment exception applies,

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:34.360
which prevents the president's pardon

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:35.610
from going into effect.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:38.890
But the Ex parte Garland
case was 150 years ago.

00:22:38.890 --> 00:22:41.940
So there's no telling that
the current Supreme Court

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:44.100
is actually even going
to take up the case.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:45.440
But the way that you would test it

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:48.880
is if a certain president pardoned himself

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:51.330
and then a new president
whether a prosecutor

00:22:51.330 --> 00:22:54.760
or the attorney general
himself decided to indict

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:57.870
and move forward with charging
that president with a crime.

00:22:57.870 --> 00:23:00.050
Well, that former president
would then be able

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:02.530
to challenge the
prosecution on the grounds

00:23:02.530 --> 00:23:05.730
that he had received and
granted his own pardon.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:07.770
And that would certainly go all the way up

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:09.970
to the Supreme Court, almost guaranteed.

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:12.580
It's possible that if
a case did make its way

00:23:12.580 --> 00:23:14.660
up to the Supreme Court and query

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:17.778
who even has standing
to bring this challenge

00:23:17.778 --> 00:23:20.270
to the president's self pardon

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:21.697
that the court would look at it and say,

00:23:21.697 --> 00:23:23.320
"look, this is a political question."

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.620
We've seen the modern Supreme Court punt

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.789
on a lot of questions claiming
this is a political question

00:23:28.789 --> 00:23:32.410
left for the legislative
and executive branches,

00:23:32.410 --> 00:23:34.040
and the separation of powers.

00:23:34.040 --> 00:23:36.790
And that there's a roundy
in the constitution

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:38.920
that the president can be impeached

00:23:38.920 --> 00:23:41.320
and removed even up to
the last day in office.

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:45.140
So if people find his behavior so odious,

00:23:45.140 --> 00:23:47.680
they can impeach him and
remove him from office.

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:51.650
And that is the political
resolution to all of these things.

00:23:51.650 --> 00:23:54.230
So there's no telling what
a Supreme Court would do,

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:56.500
whether they would follow
the guidance of the OLC,

00:23:56.500 --> 00:23:58.370
which is not binding precedent

00:23:58.370 --> 00:24:03.140
or whether they would allow
the self pardon to stand.

